{"id":"id-1792312715258","object":"chat.completion","created":1792312715,"model":"@cf/openai/gpt-oss-120b","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"**F1 Verdikt:** pending [+ S2] — **Begründung:** Derzeit kann eine Quittung nicht von einem selbst erledigten `CLOSED`‑Zustand unterschieden werden (sowohl `CLOSED` als auch `ACKED` werden als `CLOSED` geloggt). Ein eigener `ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz>`‑Akt im Ledger des Empfängers würde diesen Unterschied schaffen, jedoch wurde der frühere `xfer ack`‑Arm im Commit eb2f1b749 entfernt (‑202 Zeilen) – ein solcher Akt existiert also momentan nicht. Die zweite Frage zu `--addressed` lässt sich unabhängig beantworten; das Anzeigen geschlossener Blöcke mit einer zusätzlichen Zustandsspalte (S2) liefert die nötige Transparenz, weil `--fired` weiter 0 bleibt und Erledigtes sonst als offen erscheint. — **Riss:** Kein existierender `ACK`‑Mechanismus und keine Möglichkeit, `ACKED` von `CLOSED` zu unterscheiden; zudem bleibt `--addressed` bei geschlossenen Blöcken irreführend. **Fehlende Messungen:** Aufwand und Performance‑Impact einer Wieder‑Einführung des `ACK`‑Aktes, sowie ein Test, ob das neue S2‑Layout korrekt zwischen `ACKED` und `CLOSED` unterscheidet.  \n\n**F2 Verdikt:** B — **Begründung:** Die Messdaten zeigen, dass in einer Concurrency‑Gruppe neue Läufe wartende Vorgänger abbrechen (8 Läufe in 10 Minuten, 7 abgebrochen). Ein Gruppenschlüssel `group: register-coverage-${{ github.ref }}` mit `cancel-in-progress: true` (Option B) reduziert redundante Durchläufe und entspricht der seit 2026‑10‑08 bei","refusal":null,"annotations":null,"audio":null,"function_call":null,"reasoning":"We need to answer three questions with format:\n\nF1 Verdikt: <A|B|C|D|pending> [+ S1|S2] — Begründung: … — Riss: …\nF2 Verdikt: … — Begründung: … — Riss: …\nF3 Verdikt: … — Begründung: … — Riss: … — Zusatz: ja/nein, weil …\n\nWe must use only facts from given belegen (the prompt). No invented facts. Provide reasoning based on given info.\n\nWe have to consider each question.\n\nQuestion 1: Where is receipt (Quittung) recorded? Options A, B, C, D. Also second question about --addressed: S1 hide closed blocks, S2 list with state column. Need to give verdict and maybe indicate S1 or S2? The format says [+ S1|S2] after verdict. So we need to choose one of S1 or S2? The prompt: \"Zweitfrage `--addressed` (unabhängig): S1 geschlossene Blöcke ausblenden · S2 weiter listen, aber mit Zustandsspalte.\" So we need to give verdict for primary question and also indicate which approach for --addressed is recommended (S1 or S2). So we include [+ S1] or [+ S2].\n\nWe need to evaluate based on given facts: Currently receipt is not distinguishable; `xfer close` on foreign token is hard error; Quittung and self-completion both CLOSED not distinguishable. `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks; `--fired` remains 0, Erledigtes reads as open. Early built `xfer ack` arm was removed (commit). So options:\n\nA: own ACK act in receiver ledger, ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED. This would solve distinguishability, but requires new act and ledger entry in receiver. No evidence that such act exists; earlier ack arm was removed, implying not present. Could be reintroduced but not in current code.\n\nB: as today: receiver writes CLOSED in emitter ledger. That's current behavior, but has problem of indistinguishability.\n\nC: no new act; receipt state derived read-only from token ledger. That would keep current but maybe add logic to differentiate? Not possible because CLOSED same.\n\nD: ledger per token instead of per line, both write same storage. Not mentioned.\n\nGiven facts, the only workable solution without new code is B (status quo). But there is a strong Riss: inability to distinguish receipt vs self-completion, and `--addressed` shows closed as open. So maybe verdict pending because need measurement of impact of adding ACK act. However we have no measurement data. Could be pending.\n\nThe instruction: \"bei Unsicherheit ausdrücklich „pending“ + was zum Messen fehlt.\" If we are uncertain, we must say pending and note missing measurement.\n\nWe can decide: The best answer is A, because it solves the core problem, but we lack evidence of feasibility; we need measurement of overhead and compatibility. So we might say pending.\n\nBut we need to give a verdict. I think we can give verdict A with S2 (list closed with state column) because that would allow seeing ACKED vs CLOSED. However A introduces ACK act, which would produce ACKED state, and S2 would show state column. That seems consistent.\n\nBut we have no evidence that ACK act exists; earlier ack arm removed. So recommending A would be speculative. Might be considered a Riss: no existing ACK act.\n\nThus we should be cautious: verdict pending, need measurement of effort to reintroduce ACK act and impact on ledger size.\n\nSo F1: Verdikt: pending [+ S2] — Begründung: Aktuell kann Quittung nicht von CLOSED unterschieden werden; ein eigener ACK‑Akt im Empfänger‑Ledger würde das ermöglichen, aber ein solcher Akt wurde bereits im Commit eb2f1b749 entfernt, sodass seine Wieder‑Einführung ungetestet ist. — Riss: Fehlender ACK‑Mechanismus und keine Möglichkeit, ACK von CLOSED zu unterscheiden; zudem zeigt `--addressed` geschlossene Blöcke weiterhin als offen.\n\nWe also need to mention missing measurement: need performance impact and compatibility testing.\n\nOk.\n\nQuestion 2: Concurrency key for register-coverage. Options A per-SHA + true, B per-ref + true, C Debounce/Batch. Facts: push triggers 5-7 gates, 25 different HEAD SHAs in last 100 runs, 8 runs in 10 min, 7 cancelled. Cancel-in-progress true would cancel previous runs in same group. Currently group per ref with false, meaning runs not cancelled. Observed many cancellations already? Actually they observed 7 cancelled, meaning they already have cancel-in-progress true? Wait: they said \"letzte 100 Läufe = 25 verschiedene HEAD-SHAs; in 10 Minuten acht register-coverage-Läufe, sieben cancelled — in einer Concurrency‑Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, cancel‑in‑progress: false schützt nur den laufenden.\" This suggests currently they have group per ref with false? But they observed cancellations, maybe due to something else. The gate checks HEAD state, not per SHA artifact. Arguments: per-SHA addresses each commit uniquely, but then cancel true would cancel previous runs for same SHA (unlikely). per-ref groups runs for same branch; cancel true would keep only latest run, which seems desirable to avoid redundant runs. However concern: Kimi says cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore cancelled runs. So need to ensure gate tolerates cancellation.\n\nGiven facts, per-ref + true seems to reduce redundant runs and matches ci-gate change. The Riss: risk of inconsistent state if cancel mid-write; need to ensure gate ignores cancelled runs. No measurement of inconsistency frequency.\n\nThus verdict: B per-ref + true.\n\nAdd note: missing measurement of how often cancel mid-write leads to inconsistent register state.\n\nSo F2: Verdikt: B — Begründung: Die aktuelle Messung zeigt, dass in einer Concurrency‑Gruppe neue Läufe die wartenden Vorgänger abbrechen; ein Gruppenschlüssel pro Ref mit `cancel‑in‑progress: true` reduziert unnötige Durchläufe und entspricht der seit 2026‑10‑08 eingeführten Praxis bei `ci‑gate`. — Riss: Gefahr von inkonsistentem Register‑Zustand bei abgebrochenen Läufen; es fehlt ein Test, der bestätigt, dass das Gate korrekt ignoriert, wenn ein Lauf abgebrochen wird.\n\nQuestion 3: Register doesn't know state/stimmen. Options A, B, C, D. Need also Zusatz: must the choice itself have RAT-ID in index before built?\n\nFacts: register_lookup searches docs/... but not state/stimmen/. Measured grep count 0. So currently not included. Need to make register aware. Options:\n\nA: add state/stimmen as additional doc root. That would directly include the files.\n\nB: derive: move verdicts into handover of recipient line. That would embed them but requires architectural change.\n\nC: new register document index.\n\nD: status quo.\n\nWhich is best given facts? Adding state/stimmen as doc root is straightforward, minimal change, solves missing. No evidence of conflict. So A seems best.\n\nRiss: need to ensure that the new root is indexed and that the RAT-ID is present in index before building. The Zusatz question: Must the choice itself be a RAT-ID in the index before built? Likely yes, because architecture paths need a RAT-ID. So answer: ja, because the system requires each architectural element to have a RAT-ID before being referenced.\n\nThus F3: Verdikt: A — Begründung: `register_lookup` durchsucht nur die aufgelisteten Dokumentwurzel‑Verzeichnisse; das Hinzufügen von `state/stimmen/` als weitere Wurzel lässt die bestehenden Verdikt‑Dateien ohne weitere Migration einfließen und behebt das gemessene Fehlen (`grep -c stimmen` = 0). — Riss: Es fehlt bislang ein Index‑Eintrag für das neue Verzeichnis; ohne einen entsprechenden RAT‑ID‑Eintrag im Index wird das Register die neuen Dateien nicht finden. — Zusatz: ja, weil Architekturpfade laut R‑2026‑10‑18‑d/e eine RAT‑ID im Index benötigen, bevor sie gebaut werden.","reasoning_content":"We need to answer three questions with format:\n\nF1 Verdikt: <A|B|C|D|pending> [+ S1|S2] — Begründung: … — Riss: …\nF2 Verdikt: … — Begründung: … — Riss: …\nF3 Verdikt: … — Begründung: … — Riss: … — Zusatz: ja/nein, weil …\n\nWe must use only facts from given belegen (the prompt). No invented facts. Provide reasoning based on given info.\n\nWe have to consider each question.\n\nQuestion 1: Where is receipt (Quittung) recorded? Options A, B, C, D. Also second question about --addressed: S1 hide closed blocks, S2 list with state column. Need to give verdict and maybe indicate S1 or S2? The format says [+ S1|S2] after verdict. So we need to choose one of S1 or S2? The prompt: \"Zweitfrage `--addressed` (unabhängig): S1 geschlossene Blöcke ausblenden · S2 weiter listen, aber mit Zustandsspalte.\" So we need to give verdict for primary question and also indicate which approach for --addressed is recommended (S1 or S2). So we include [+ S1] or [+ S2].\n\nWe need to evaluate based on given facts: Currently receipt is not distinguishable; `xfer close` on foreign token is hard error; Quittung and self-completion both CLOSED not distinguishable. `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks; `--fired` remains 0, Erledigtes reads as open. Early built `xfer ack` arm was removed (commit). So options:\n\nA: own ACK act in receiver ledger, ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED. This would solve distinguishability, but requires new act and ledger entry in receiver. No evidence that such act exists; earlier ack arm was removed, implying not present. Could be reintroduced but not in current code.\n\nB: as today: receiver writes CLOSED in emitter ledger. That's current behavior, but has problem of indistinguishability.\n\nC: no new act; receipt state derived read-only from token ledger. That would keep current but maybe add logic to differentiate? Not possible because CLOSED same.\n\nD: ledger per token instead of per line, both write same storage. Not mentioned.\n\nGiven facts, the only workable solution without new code is B (status quo). But there is a strong Riss: inability to distinguish receipt vs self-completion, and `--addressed` shows closed as open. So maybe verdict pending because need measurement of impact of adding ACK act. However we have no measurement data. Could be pending.\n\nThe instruction: \"bei Unsicherheit ausdrücklich „pending“ + was zum Messen fehlt.\" If we are uncertain, we must say pending and note missing measurement.\n\nWe can decide: The best answer is A, because it solves the core problem, but we lack evidence of feasibility; we need measurement of overhead and compatibility. So we might say pending.\n\nBut we need to give a verdict. I think we can give verdict A with S2 (list closed with state column) because that would allow seeing ACKED vs CLOSED. However A introduces ACK act, which would produce ACKED state, and S2 would show state column. That seems consistent.\n\nBut we have no evidence that ACK act exists; earlier ack arm removed. So recommending A would be speculative. Might be considered a Riss: no existing ACK act.\n\nThus we should be cautious: verdict pending, need measurement of effort to reintroduce ACK act and impact on ledger size.\n\nSo F1: Verdikt: pending [+ S2] — Begründung: Aktuell kann Quittung nicht von CLOSED unterschieden werden; ein eigener ACK‑Akt im Empfänger‑Ledger würde das ermöglichen, aber ein solcher Akt wurde bereits im Commit eb2f1b749 entfernt, sodass seine Wieder‑Einführung ungetestet ist. — Riss: Fehlender ACK‑Mechanismus und keine Möglichkeit, ACK von CLOSED zu unterscheiden; zudem zeigt `--addressed` geschlossene Blöcke weiterhin als offen.\n\nWe also need to mention missing measurement: need performance impact and compatibility testing.\n\nOk.\n\nQuestion 2: Concurrency key for register-coverage. Options A per-SHA + true, B per-ref + true, C Debounce/Batch. Facts: push triggers 5-7 gates, 25 different HEAD SHAs in last 100 runs, 8 runs in 10 min, 7 cancelled. Cancel-in-progress true would cancel previous runs in same group. Currently group per ref with false, meaning runs not cancelled. Observed many cancellations already? Actually they observed 7 cancelled, meaning they already have cancel-in-progress true? Wait: they said \"letzte 100 Läufe = 25 verschiedene HEAD-SHAs; in 10 Minuten acht register-coverage-Läufe, sieben cancelled — in einer Concurrency‑Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, cancel‑in‑progress: false schützt nur den laufenden.\" This suggests currently they have group per ref with false? But they observed cancellations, maybe due to something else. The gate checks HEAD state, not per SHA artifact. Arguments: per-SHA addresses each commit uniquely, but then cancel true would cancel previous runs for same SHA (unlikely). per-ref groups runs for same branch; cancel true would keep only latest run, which seems desirable to avoid redundant runs. However concern: Kimi says cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore cancelled runs. So need to ensure gate tolerates cancellation.\n\nGiven facts, per-ref + true seems to reduce redundant runs and matches ci-gate change. The Riss: risk of inconsistent state if cancel mid-write; need to ensure gate ignores cancelled runs. No measurement of inconsistency frequency.\n\nThus verdict: B per-ref + true.\n\nAdd note: missing measurement of how often cancel mid-write leads to inconsistent register state.\n\nSo F2: Verdikt: B — Begründung: Die aktuelle Messung zeigt, dass in einer Concurrency‑Gruppe neue Läufe die wartenden Vorgänger abbrechen; ein Gruppenschlüssel pro Ref mit `cancel‑in‑progress: true` reduziert unnötige Durchläufe und entspricht der seit 2026‑10‑08 eingeführten Praxis bei `ci‑gate`. — Riss: Gefahr von inkonsistentem Register‑Zustand bei abgebrochenen Läufen; es fehlt ein Test, der bestätigt, dass das Gate korrekt ignoriert, wenn ein Lauf abgebrochen wird.\n\nQuestion 3: Register doesn't know state/stimmen. Options A, B, C, D. Need also Zusatz: must the choice itself have RAT-ID in index before built?\n\nFacts: register_lookup searches docs/... but not state/stimmen/. Measured grep count 0. So currently not included. Need to make register aware. Options:\n\nA: add state/stimmen as additional doc root. That would directly include the files.\n\nB: derive: move verdicts into handover of recipient line. That would embed them but requires architectural change.\n\nC: new register document index.\n\nD: status quo.\n\nWhich is best given facts? Adding state/stimmen as doc root is straightforward, minimal change, solves missing. No evidence of conflict. So A seems best.\n\nRiss: need to ensure that the new root is indexed and that the RAT-ID is present in index before building. The Zusatz question: Must the choice itself be a RAT-ID in the index before built? Likely yes, because architecture paths need a RAT-ID. So answer: ja, because the system requires each architectural element to have a RAT-ID before being referenced.\n\nThus F3: Verdikt: A — Begründung: `register_lookup` durchsucht nur die aufgelisteten Dokumentwurzel‑Verzeichnisse; das Hinzufügen von `state/stimmen/` als weitere Wurzel lässt die bestehenden Verdikt‑Dateien ohne weitere Migration einfließen und behebt das gemessene Fehlen (`grep -c stimmen` = 0). — Riss: Es fehlt bislang ein Index‑Eintrag für das neue Verzeichnis; ohne einen entsprechenden RAT‑ID‑Eintrag im Index wird das Register die neuen Dateien nicht finden. — Zusatz: ja, weil Architekturpfade laut R‑2026‑10‑18‑d/e eine RAT‑ID im Index benötigen, bevor sie gebaut werden."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"length","stop_reason":null,"token_ids":null,"routed_experts":null}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1192,"completion_tokens":2200,"total_tokens":3392,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0},"neurons":187.927001953125}}